   "text": "The image shows a Pt\u00b2\u207a ion, two carboxylate anions and one trans-1,2-diaminocyclohexane, drawn as separate species with no bonds to the metal.\n\n**Carboxylates.** Each \u207bOOC group is followed by three drawn chain vertices and then an n-decyl group (10 carbons). That gives 1 + 3 + 10 = 14 carbons, so each anion is tetradecanoate (myristate): `[O-]C(=O)` plus 13 CH2/CH3 carbons.\n\n**Diamine.** The cyclohexane ring has six vertices. Two adjacent ring carbons each carry an NH2: one on a wedge and one on a hash, so the two amines are trans. Assigning CIP priorities (N > ring carbon bearing N > other ring carbon > H):\n- The wedged carbon is R.\n- The hashed carbon is also R.\n\nThe diamine is therefore (1R,2R), written as `N[C@@H]1CCCC[C@H]1N`. I checked this SMILES by hand against the drawing, and both centres come out R.\n\nSMILES: [Pt+2].[O-]C(=O)CCCCCCCCCCCCC.[O-]C(=O)CCCCCCCCCCCCC.N[C@@H]1CCCC[C@H]1N"
